WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.540
My talk today is going to be
about project immortality.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.300
It's about using GitHub to
make your work live forever.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.150
You can follow me on Twitter
at underscore TanHo. Slides

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.400
are-- this is actually
a GitHub repository,

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.400
but the slides are
uploaded to that.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.640
So I'd like to start by.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:23.230
Wait a second.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.000
Clicker yes, we like clickers.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.500
So I'd like to introduce you
to the life and times of a data

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.602
science project.

00:00:44.602 --> 00:00:46.310
This might sound
familiar to most of you.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.102
Certainly the story of
most of my projects,

00:00:48.102 --> 00:00:49.280
if not all of them.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.990
You start by
importing some data,

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.930
whether that's
from scraping APIs,

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:57.200
a database, pre-existing data,
stuff that your company has,

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.155
whatever it is.

00:00:58.155 --> 00:00:59.530
You start by
importing some data.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:00.600
Then you get to know this data.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.470
You do some wrangling.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.520
You do some feature engineering.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.478
You start doing some
exploratory data analysis.

00:01:04.478 --> 00:01:06.320
Try to understand
how messy the data is

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:07.440
and how bad your data is.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.020
Shout out to Jim.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.163
And then, once you've kind
of done that,

00:01:11.163 --> 00:01:12.830
kind of understand
that data little bit,

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:14.330
as from a human
perspective, you start

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.200
to try to teach your computer
to do something with it.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.575
So you throw it
into a model, you

00:01:17.575 --> 00:01:20.090
try to get some clustering
done or regressions

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.550
or try to predict
a number, and then

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.760
you study the results of
that output, you plot it,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.980
and then you tweet the plot.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.882
So, you know, you share
it with the world.

00:01:29.882 --> 00:01:31.090
You've learned something new.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:32.350
Here's what here's
what I've learned.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:32.990
Here's, you know,

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.170
get some feedback on it.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.270
Validation, 15 minutes of fame,

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.700
hopefully. Hopefully
everyone likes it.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.110
You get feedback, and then

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.870
most projects die here, right?

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.830
And it's rightfully so because
you've learned something new.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.580
You've you've done
what you set out to do.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.372
You've learned something
from that project,

00:01:50.372 --> 00:01:51.700
and you're ready to move on.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:52.910
Sometime later,

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.450
this happens.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.050
Relevant XKCD as ever.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.800
Never have I ever felt
so close to another soul

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.490
and yet so helplessly alone
as when I Google this problem

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.820
and there's one result
a thread by someone

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.470
who studied the exact same
problem that I'm interested in,

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.240
and it was last
posted to in 2020.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.630
2020 feels like 22,003, right?

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:14.450
Who were you,

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:15.800
DenverCoder9? Who were you?

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.210
What did you see?

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.740
What did you learn?

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.460
This story happens a lot, right?

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.480
And whether it's a Stack Overflow
answer or a problem, a GitHub,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.060
sometimes now you'll Google and
you'll find a Twitter thread.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.390
How do you help this person?

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.700
Actually, I'm not
that interested in how

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.290
you help that person because
the answer is they'll

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.160
reach out to you if
you're available.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.112
You'll spend time and energy
and you'll give it back to them

00:02:36.112 --> 00:02:36.820
if you have time.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.850
Right? What I'm actually
more interested in

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.410
is how can your project
help this person?

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.570
Because that's not
always possible.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:46.140
Whether you're constrained,
where your time has limits,

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.480
your energy has limits.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.210
Your project should be able
to help them and get what

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.800
they need from it and move on.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.223
So my, my
question, my talk today

00:02:55.223 --> 00:02:56.640
is going be about
how can you help

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.120
your project help this person.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.210
And I think that there's.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:02.450
I skipped slide on me.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.870
That GitHub has a bunch of
resources that can really

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:08.400
help you along this path.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.450
So, who this talk is for?

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.040
This talk is for people who are
doing personal hobby projects.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.170
It's for people who are
doing academic research.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.378
It's for people who are
interested in public and open

00:03:17.378 --> 00:03:19.267
source work. More broadly,

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:21.100
it's for people who
don't have a budget when

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.142
they're doing their work.

00:03:22.142 --> 00:03:26.260
And people who are interested
in helping others use their work

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.870
and helping their
projects live on.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.490
So why do I care
so much about this?

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:31.060
This is me.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.560
This is literally
my profile picture

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.530
on every single Twitter and
Twitch and everything else

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.510
about me.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:39.520
In a past life, I was
a property manager,

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.270
grew up in the family business,
absolutely hated that job.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:46.370
So, on the side I started
doing fantasy football.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:50.365
Started analyzing it,
started teaching myself

00:03:50.365 --> 00:03:51.740
some data analysis
to go with it.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.220
Started with Excel
power query, eventually

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.010
learned R, taught myself
basically football analysis,

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.770
NFL fantasy football,
et cetera. Eventually

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.500
that led to a data
science career.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.620
I got a job as in a home-building
company doing data science

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:06.945
and doing programming.

00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:08.320
And eventually
and recently, I've

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:09.970
just accepted a position
with Zealous Analytics

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.000
to work on pro soccer.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.510
So, you know, you can
turn, you can

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:16.810
make that career switch
if you're interested by doing

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.040
this sort of thing.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.200
And then today along
the way, I've, I've

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.600
become a maintainer
of public NFL data

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.030
and I maintain nflverse and
ffverse R packages

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.520
for NFL and fantasy
football, respectively.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.090
So why do I care
so much about this?

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:30.920
I've been the
hobbyist programmer.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.670
I've been the person who's
broke and looking for wanting

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:34.970
to do stuff without a budget.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:38.230
And I care today even
still about making sure

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.410
that people can build on
my work and can use my work

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:43.670
in their own projects.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.200
So let's talk about one of
my projects, ffopportunity.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.440
So it's a project that
uses NFL play-by-play data

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:50.122
to study expected
fantasy points.

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:51.830
Now, I'm not going to
go into the details

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:53.618
about NFL or expected fantasy points.

00:04:53.618 --> 00:04:55.410
You don't need to know
anything about that.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.680
But if you're interested,
expected fantasy points

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.570
help measure the value
of play opportunities

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.737
in fantasy football and you
can find my project here

00:05:01.737 --> 00:05:04.948
GitHub dot com ffverse
ffopportunity. I'll take you

00:05:04.948 --> 00:05:06.240
through the structure anyway, though.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.450
So this might sound familiar.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.980
You start by importing some
nflverse play-by-play data.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.360
Then I wrangled some
features together.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.980
Trained an XGBoost
model and then used it,

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.460
used that model, to
predict fantasy points.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.710
Right? This is story of every
machine learning project.

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:23.670
Every data science project,

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:24.320
really.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.790
Now what?

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:28.622
How could I make
ffopportunity live on?

00:05:28.622 --> 00:05:30.330
I'm actually going to
frame this question

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.300
what is this person who
stumbles on my project

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.520
later interested in? And
what kind of questions

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.520
are they going to be asking?

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.760
How can they use and
improve on my model?

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:42.480
No model is perfect,
but they're useful.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.960
And then understanding
how past work

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.630
has understanding the past
work in that field can

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.110
help you improve on that model.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:51.652
Where the gaps are,
understand how to use it,

00:05:51.652 --> 00:05:52.860
understand how to improve it.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.287
Where to get that kind of thing.

00:05:56.287 --> 00:05:58.120
Can new predictions be
automated every week?

00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:59.130
Maybe they're good
with this model,

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.388
but they're interested in
how the prediction, what

00:06:01.388 --> 00:06:03.180
the predictions are
and what the model says

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.722
about games that
haven't happened yet

00:06:04.722 --> 00:06:06.450
and games that are
going to happen soon.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.180
So can you automate new
predictions every week

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:10.120
of the NFL season?

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:14.250
There's a new slate of games
every week from September

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.690
through to January, February.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:18.890
Lastly, where can they
find these predictions?

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:19.800
Where can I put it?

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.000
Where can they access
the predictions

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.950
in a way that's
not trying to ask

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:27.173
me to email them or tweet them
or send them these predictions?

00:06:27.173 --> 00:06:28.840
It's no good if it
lives on my computer.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.090
Right? So let's go through
these questions one by one.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.900
Can someone use and
improve on my model?

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.250
There's a lot of
work in the R space now

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.020
in terms of reproducibility.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.200
Renv is a great option.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.450
Docker is a great option.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:43.000
The one I actually like to
use is making it a package

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.187
and I'm not talking
about making it for CRAN,

00:06:45.187 --> 00:06:47.020
but I think there's two
elements of packages

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.437
that I think are
really underrated

00:06:48.437 --> 00:06:50.730
and help kind of build
things going forward.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.450
One of them is adding
a description file

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:55.130
and the other one is
wrapping code into functions.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.080
Adding a description file.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.270
One of the things that
addind a description file does,

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:01.890
it forces you or asks
you to add a license.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.490
And a license is really
important, especially

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.010
when you're doing
public work because it

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:09.687
tells people what you're OK
with them doing with your work.

00:07:09.687 --> 00:07:11.270
Right? So if you want
them to if you're

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:13.228
OK with doing it, them
doing anything with that,

00:07:13.228 --> 00:07:15.000
say so. Add an MIT license.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.210
That's what that's
basically they're for.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.070
If you want them to
only use this work

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.350
to help extend public
work, you can use a license

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:23.010
like GPL v three, right?

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.860
So just choosing
the license helps

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.350
communicate that you want
them to use that work.

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:30.140
And by sharing that work
to them with that license,

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.210
people can understand
what you're OK

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.020
with them doing with it.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.450
The other thing that it does
is it makes dependencies.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:38.550
It makes installation of
dependencies really easy

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.840
because you're forced to
list all of the dependencies.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.250
And if you're able to also
list them in versions,

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:48.210
it makes things like remotes
or pakc or pacman work

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.610
because they can read the
description file from GitHub,

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:53.340
and then install all
the dependencies for you

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:55.390
while you're installing
this package.

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:56.260
For this package.

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:58.260
And even if it doesn't
have any functions in it,

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:00.093
it'll still install the
dependencies for you

00:08:00.093 --> 00:08:03.600
and then you can use that in
running the script after you've

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:04.920
cloned it on GitHub.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.420
Of course, usethis
has this covered,

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:08.910
so you don't actually need to
type out a description file.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:11.068
It's got four functions
that you need to do

00:08:11.068 --> 00:08:12.360
exactly what I'm talking about.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.872
Use description, use
MIT license, use package

00:08:14.872 --> 00:08:16.330
for each package
that you're using.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:18.455
Then once you've added all
the package you use, use

00:08:18.455 --> 00:08:20.513
latest dependencies and
it's going to version

00:08:20.513 --> 00:08:22.180
lock the version
that's on your machine.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:25.680
So it'll put on, put
that in as the version.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.360
And then you've got, you, got
you set this up to for success.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.610
The other thing that I like
to do, this is kind of the next level,

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:35.700
is to wrap logic into functions.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.320
It's really easy for users.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.360
So from your end of
things, what you need to do

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:41.485
is convert the hard-coded
variables and data frames

00:08:41.485 --> 00:08:43.419
to arguments, extract functions.

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:46.690
A cool feature in RStudio,
control Control Alt X.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:50.340
And then if you copy and
paste or if you select

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:53.250
the entire code chunk and
you press this shortcut,

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:54.940
I think it's Command Option X.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.660
I don't use a Mac,
but it's related.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.510
It'll automatically
create a function

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:02.610
and put the skeleton of
your code in a function

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:05.230
and take all the variables
and put them into arguments.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:06.980
So this already basically
does it for you.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.480
And then the other thing you
can do is add some usage notes.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:12.030
I don't say documentation here because
documentation scares people,

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:13.980
but really understanding
how you use

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.010
logic, how you use the function
and what the function is meant

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:18.977
to do will help
other people kind of

00:09:18.977 --> 00:09:20.560
understand where
you're going with it.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.100
And the goal is to make it
easy for users to run, right?

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.882
So R users love functions.

00:09:25.882 --> 00:09:27.340
They understand
exactly what to do

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:29.673
when you give them a function,
you put arguments into it

00:09:29.673 --> 00:09:31.800
and then you kind
of go from there.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.652
OK. So we talked about packages.

00:09:33.652 --> 00:09:35.110
That's all that's
the bare minimum.

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:36.735
There's a whole bunch
more in packages.

00:09:36.735 --> 00:09:38.810
There's a whole book on it.

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:41.327
But from my end of things,
that's the bare minimum.

00:09:41.327 --> 00:09:42.910
The next thing I'm
going to talk about

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:45.230
is how can you predict
new automation?

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:47.410
Can you automate
predictions every week?

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:51.302
Right? Because the users who
don't care about the model

00:09:51.302 --> 00:09:53.260
itself are really only
interested in the output

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:53.810
of the model.

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:57.350
So can you automate giving
them a model every single week?

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.200
The answer is yes, of course.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:01.570
With GitHub Actions.
This is literally

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:05.260
the 20-line file that automates
scripts, the automates that

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:08.440
automates the ffopportunity
predictions,

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:10.450
and it uses the description
file that we just

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:14.150
talked about to run every
night after every game.

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:16.690
So that's four or
5:00 AM on Monday,

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:19.180
Tuesday and Friday from
September to January.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.770
And then it installs
R on the machine.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:25.180
The virtual machine installs
the R packages from the dependencies

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:27.967
that I listed in my description,
and then it runs a script.

00:10:27.967 --> 00:10:28.550
And that's it.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.100
That's all you need to do.

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:32.440
So you're obviously there's
a whole bunch of magic that

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:33.607
happens in the backend here.

00:10:33.607 --> 00:10:35.230
But from an end
user perspective,

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:36.730
as someone automating
your projects,

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:39.700
this is already this is you're
leaning on a bunch of work

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:43.020
by the r-lib actions
team that is out there

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:44.270
and it's ready for you to use.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:46.010
Right? And GitHub
actions is free.

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:48.850
If you've got public
work, public projects,

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:50.350
there's no action limits,

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:53.350
action minutes limits.

00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:55.372
That sounded weird anyways.

00:10:55.372 --> 00:10:57.830
The last question and the one
I think that more people need

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:01.160
to know about is, where can
I make predictions accessible?

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.630
So if you're already we're
already talking about GitHub.

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:05.630
So the common
approach is to just

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:08.325
commit things to your
GitHub repository, right?

00:11:08.325 --> 00:11:09.450
Well, there's three things,

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:11.450
there's three common
problems with that.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:13.042
I'll list I'll talk
about them here.

00:11:13.042 --> 00:11:14.750
File size limits,
inefficient binary data

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:16.125
storage, and commit
history chaos.

00:11:16.125 --> 00:11:18.390
We'll start with
file size limits.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:21.000
This is approximate, but
I think the GitHub file

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.340
size limits are something
like 75 megabytes per file

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:24.830
and 2 gigabytes per repo.

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:27.900
In today's modern age, that's
basically nothing, right?

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:30.160
So it's very easy
to go through this.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:31.620
So if you have a
CSV, for example,

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:33.960
you might think,
OK, well, maybe what

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:38.670
I should do is use an RDS file
or a parquet file to use arrow,

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.290
or whatever kind
of small file size.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.090
I think my friend Seb
he likes to use QS.

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:45.730
Cuz same idea.

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:48.000
They're all binary files.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.670
So the problem with that
is that version control

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:54.090
is incredibly bad at
dealing with binary files.

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.250
Here's an example
of a repository that

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:59.370
has a daily automation
that basically overwrites

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.140
an RDS file every single day.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:02.020
It's really tiny.

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.510
So I've annotated for you.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.490
The actual data
in this repository

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.740
is 60 megabytes in size.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:10.530
And then Git, since
I think this is

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.810
over the course of
six months or a year,

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:16.770
has automatically backed up
six gigabytes worth of data

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:19.140
that I didn't know
about until I decided

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:22.110
that I need to clone it down
and do something with the code.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.660
So and then once
I closed it down,

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:26.970
you can't push it back
to GitHub because Git

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:29.400
has a limit of two gigabytes.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:33.630
So I actually like rage
tweeted a whole bunch of this stuff

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:34.480
when I found this out.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:36.630
So if you really if
you're really keen,

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:38.250
you can go find it.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:39.710
So it's incredibly bad.

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:43.470
And what's happening is that
Git version control is literally

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:44.530
tracking versions,

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:46.770
right? And what it does is
it compares the differences

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:48.250
and tries to log
the differences.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:52.110
But binary files have or
are entirely different

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:53.230
every time you write it.

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:56.020
So it naturally stores a copy.

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:58.510
Right? And that's why Git
is really bad at dealing

00:12:58.510 --> 00:12:59.260
with binary files.

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:02.580
So even if you're into parquet and
the latest and greatest there,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.610
it's very quick, especially
when you pair it with actions,

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:08.040
that you kill your
repo like this.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:09.753
Lastly, this is another problem.

00:13:09.753 --> 00:13:12.420
If you're committing to the same
people that your code lives in,

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:14.790
you end up with 6,000 commits.

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:17.760
Now pop quiz which
commit did I, did I,

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.380
did I make a typo
in and automatically

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:20.380
write to the wrong file?

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:22.560
Well, it was like
4,222 commits ago.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:23.970
How do I revert to that?

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:25.860
Right? It's impossible.

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:28.500
So commit history chaos.

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:30.810
There's obviously
better solutions.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.390
Here are some meh solutions.

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:35.310
There is Git Large
File Storage, takes

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.770
care of some of the
large file size problems,

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:39.760
but it also wrecks
your Git repository.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:41.790
It's impossible to
collaborate with.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:43.530
It's really hard
for users to work

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:45.900
with because your data isn't
actually stored on GitHub

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:46.950
in files anymore.

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:48.420
Sharded everywhere.

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:50.320
Amazon S3 buckets, two problems.

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:51.763
One, they cost money.

00:13:51.763 --> 00:13:54.180
Again, we're talking about
free, especially in sustainable

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:54.680
projects.

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:58.625
And the other problem
is that it's stored away

00:13:58.625 --> 00:13:59.500
from your repository.

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:03.150
So you have no idea what data is
available for that repository.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:05.680
And so someone's got this bucket
and it's got things on it.

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:09.035
It's really hard to work with
visually for the novice user,

00:14:09.035 --> 00:14:10.410
someone who's not
using accessing

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:13.170
a terminal in a
programming perspective

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:14.650
to understand what
data is there.

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:17.790
Lastly, people still use Dropbox
for this sort of problem,

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.200
but that's also really
difficult to work with.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:22.770
But now from the command line,
say, how do you get files

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:24.450
back and forth from there?

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:27.900
The real solution again, pairing
with the GitHub theme GitHub

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.550
Releases.

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:33.032
And GitHub Releases is it's
kind of a lesser known thing,

00:14:33.032 --> 00:14:35.490
but you can make a new release
and releases are stored right

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:36.450
next to your repo.

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:39.300
So if you go through this
section beside your repo,

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:41.900
there's releases. You
make a new release,

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:44.400
you upload files to the release,
and then when you're ready,

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.587
you can update those
files as desired.

00:14:46.587 --> 00:14:48.420
How is this different
from the other GitHub

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:49.780
data repos?

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:51.630
Well, there's friendly
file size limits.

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:53.760
So each file that you
upload to a release

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:55.210
is understood to be bigger.

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:58.410
So the actual limit for a
file is like two gigabytes,

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:01.230
and you can upload as many
of these files as you want.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:04.500
So that's much more generous
than two gigabytes in total

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.120
and 75 megabytes. You
version by choice.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.330
So when you upload,
you overwrite the file

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:11.150
unless you make a
new release, right?

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:12.960
And if you're ready
to archive that data,

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.270
you make any release, upload
to that new repository

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.460
and you still have
the old version.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:20.070
It keeps your commit history clean.
You're manually uploading or uploading

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.390
through the API. Data
stays with your project.

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:23.910
So if I'm interested
in what data

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.047
is available within
the nflverse data package,

00:15:26.047 --> 00:15:28.630
you go through the releases, you
figure it out. You can point

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:29.730
and click and download.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:33.270
And best of all, it's free
both for public and private

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:35.270
repositories.

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:37.580
Of course, R is
wonderful, and there's

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:39.230
a package for everything.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:41.780
The piggyback package, which
I started working with,

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:44.300
and it's originally
by Carl, I'm gonna

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:48.800
this, Boettiger came up with
three functions that basically

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:49.960
run that whole process.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:54.000
First is PB release create.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:55.240
You create a new release.

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:57.960
PB upload to upload a file
to a specific release.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:15:59.910
PB download lets you
download all the files,

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:03.860
some of the files, or just
one file from that release.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:06.590
So to recap, today I
presented three tools

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:07.895
for project immortality.

00:16:07.895 --> 00:16:09.770
Talked about R package
infrastructure to help

00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:11.090
make things easy
to install and also

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:13.465
to communicate what you want
people to do with your work.

00:16:13.465 --> 00:16:16.010
Talked about GitHub Actions, to
schedule and automate things.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:19.040
And then GitHub Releases as
an awesome and wonderful way

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:20.560
to store data.

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:23.590
So with that, with those
three tools under your belt,

00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:25.920
I'd like to leave you
with one last question.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.920
How can you help your
projects help others,

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:31.790
now and into the future?

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:33.550
Thank you.

